{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-29637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}$ for $-\\pi <x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-29637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add or subtract integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\pi =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\pi =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-29637" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7f6a0e07852f02daa7a18e474c058be06cc00f22cd7d1d37072eb8e639f14aff"} {"question" "q-29637" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-29637" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{11{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"] ["math" "-\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"] ["math" "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{12}"] ["math" "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-29637" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-29664" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x<{180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-29664" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{4}{3})=(-53.130\\,1…)^{∘}≈-{53.130}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-{53.130}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-{53.130}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add and subtract integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+x={180}^{∘}-{53.130}^{∘}={126.870}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-{53.130}^{∘},{126.870}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n-53.130\\cdot 126.870≈-6\\,741\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-29664" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7f6a0e07852f02daa7a18e474c058be06cc00f22cd7d1d37072eb8e639f14aff"} {"question" "q-29664" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-29664" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-6\\,561"] ["math" "-6\\,631"] ["math" "-6\\,731"] ["math" "-6\\,741"] ["math" "-6\\,851"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-29664" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-6\\,741"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-76930" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{5}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-76930" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=5\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\text{arctan}(5)=1.373\\,400…≈1.373\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.373\\,4$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.373\\,4.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=\\pi +{x}_{1}=\\pi +1.373\\,4=4.515\\,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n1.373\\,4\\cdot 4.515\\,0≈6.2\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-76930" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7f6a0e07852f02daa7a18e474c058be06cc00f22cd7d1d37072eb8e639f14aff"} {"question" "q-76930" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-76930" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5.8"] ["math" "5.9"] ["math" "6.2"] ["math" "6.4"] ["math" "6.8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-76930" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6.2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-120646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $7-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7$ for ${0}^{∘}<x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-120646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7 \\\\ -4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={270}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x={90}^{∘},{270}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n90\\cdot 270=24\\,300.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-120646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7f6a0e07852f02daa7a18e474c058be06cc00f22cd7d1d37072eb8e639f14aff"} {"question" "q-120646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-120646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "16\\,200"] ["math" "18\\,900"] ["math" "24\\,300"] ["math" "27\\,000"] ["math" "37\\,800"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-120646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "24\\,300"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-330708" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the two solutions to the equation $\\sqrt{8-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}=\\sqrt{8}$ for $0<x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-330708" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{8-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\ 8-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =8 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-330708" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7f6a0e07852f02daa7a18e474c058be06cc00f22cd7d1d37072eb8e639f14aff"} {"question" "q-330708" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-330708" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-330708" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"} {"question" "q-330708" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981870/state.json" "file_sha256" "f411625312f94d26bc526edbeb1a597eb129e762937c310a99fba6906566f62a"}] "usage" {"q-29637" []} "basis" 900 "retractions" [[17592186115634 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nGiven that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\cot x = \\sqrt{3}$ for $- π < x < π$, what is the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$?"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186113205 17592186113206 17592186113207 17592186113208 17592186113209] "immutable_fields" [[17592186113204 :answer-field/correct] [17592186113204 :answer-field/id] [17592186113204 :answer-field/key] [17592186113204 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
